  "worked_solution": "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $3$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\u222c}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{\u0394}\\text{\u00a0}\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )\\,r\\text{\u00a0}drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066271/assets/4f7b1647d893512605ee1a2e9cee3e5511a00dc7ad9b1765fff1d0ba0710895f.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\u0394={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{\u0394}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(81-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =162[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =162(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=81\\pi .$",
